 ],
 "literal": "Aches my heart, aches my heart! / Of the dry land, the densely grown, small-leaved nerunji's / eye-pleasing new flower yielded thorns, just like / pleasant things did our lover, / doing unpleasant things now, aches my heart!",
 "translation": "My heart aches, O my heart aches! Just like the small-leaved nerunji plant growing in the dry fields, whose beautiful new flowers, so pleasing to the eye, end up yielding sharp thorns, my lover, who once did such sweet and pleasant things, now brings me only pain. My heart aches!",
 "modern": "என் நெஞ்சம் வலிக்கிறது, என் நெஞ்சம் வலிக்கிறது! புன்புலத்தில் அடர்ந்து வளர்ந்த சிறிய இலைகளைக் கொண்ட நெருஞ்சிச் செடியின், கண்ணுக்கு இனிய புதிய மலர் பின்னர் கூர்மையான முட்களைத் தருவது போல, முன்பு எனக்கு இனியவற்றைச் செய்த நம் காதலர், இப்போது துன்பம் தருவது என் நெஞ்சை வலிக்கச் செய்கிறது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the marutam (agricultural) landscape, where the theme is infidelity and the heroine's sulking. The speaker is the heroine, expressing her deep grief to herself about the hero's changed behavior. She compares her lover to the nerunji (puncture vine) flower, which is beautiful and bright yellow to look at, but eventually produces sharp, painful thorns. Similarly, the hero was once incredibly loving and sweet, but has now turned cold and unfaithful, causing her immense pain. This vivid natural imagery perfectly captures the transition from the joy of early love to the sharp sting of betrayal. Key words include nōm (aches), நெருஞ்சி (puncture vine), and innā (unpleasant things).",
